From 62efbac1fe3130086b45d43e4064f7142a6c9a6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 14:58:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Add a persistent label mask cache for the pango backend

Every start with the pango text backend paid for the fontconfig scan and the
shaping of every label, which takes seconds on a 200 MHz handheld, and did the
same work again at the next start. The masks only depend on the layout, the
keyboard size, the scale and the font, so they are kept in one file per
combination (cache.h describes the format) that later starts map read-only.

The reader checks the header, the checksum and every offset and size against
the size of the file before it hands out a single pointer, and anything wrong
is a miss: the labels are rendered and the file is written again. The writer
builds the file in memory (sorted index, equal masks stored once, nothing that
varies between runs, so the same input gives the same bytes), writes it to a
temporary file in the same directory and renames it into place, so a reader
sees the old file or the new one and a failed write changes nothing.

On a first start the visible layer is rendered at once, as before. The other
layers are rendered from the main loop's poll timeout, two labels per wake-up
after 10 ms of quiet, and the file is written once when they are all there.
--prerender does the same work in one go, needs no compositor, and exits
non-zero with a message if it cannot write the file. --cache-dir and --no-cache
choose or disable the directory (default /var/cache/wvkbd-ipaq, changed at
build time with the cache_dir meson option). The bitmap backend has no cache
and ignores the options.

The cache_tool meson option builds wvkbd-cache-gen with the native toolchain.
It writes the files for 240x320, 320x240, 480x640 and 640x480 so a target need
not run pango where the same libraries and font are installed (see below);
they are what --prerender writes for the same screen. For
that the geometry code (kbd_init_layout and friends) moved from keyboard.c to
geometry.c and the icon and label box helpers from drw.c to label.c, since
neither needs Wayland. The label boxes are computed in one place for drawing
and for the cache: with a fractional scale the key popup, drawn one keyboard
height higher, rounds its edges differently, so the cache also holds the popup
boxes when the popup is on. An empty label is no longer sent to the backend.

The masks also depend on what pango resolves the font description to, which
the description does not say: a font package update replaces the file and
would leave the old masks in use for good. The key (the header and the
file name, as a hash) therefore includes the versions of pango, cairo,
harfbuzz and fontconfig and the path, size and modification time of the
font file, found through pango_fc_font_get_pattern when pangoft2 and
fontconfig can be linked (they are what pango itself needs, so the meson
build only warns when their development files are missing and then keys on
the library versions alone; the Makefile build needs them). Only the first
face of the description is followed, not the fallback fonts pango picks per
glyph. Finding the file starts fontconfig, so a start with a usable cache
still pays for that. Files made by wvkbd-cache-gen on another machine are
only used where the same versions and font file are installed. A changed
key is a miss and the new file is written beside the old one.

--prerender sizes the keyboard with the same function as the running
keyboard (pick_geometry in main.c). That width is the screen width unless
the keyboard is docked, because a full-width layer surface is sized by the
compositor, which overrides --width; before, --prerender counted --width
without --dock and wrote a file no start ever looked for.

The man page and the README describe the options, the first-start behaviour,
the cache key and the file format.
