+# LABEL CACHE
+
+Rendering the labels with pango costs a fontconfig scan and the shaping of
+every label, which is slow on a small machine. The pango text backend
+therefore renders each label once into a coverage mask and keeps all masks of
+one keyboard size in a file that a later start maps read-only instead of
+rendering again. The bitmap text backend has built-in fonts and no cache.
+
+The file is made by whichever of these happens first:
+
+*First start.* The visible layer is rendered synchronously, as the keyboard
+needs it at once. The other layers are rendered while the keyboard is idle:
+when nothing has happened for 10 ms, two labels are rendered, and so on until
+all are done, so a key press is never kept waiting for more than two labels.
+Then the file is written, once.
+
+*--prerender*, which does the same work in one go and then exits.
+
+*wvkbd-cache-gen*, a program built on the build machine when the meson option
+_cache_tool_ is on (it needs _text=pango_ and is not installed). It writes the
+files for the screens 240x320, 320x240, 480x640 and 640x480 into a directory
+given as its argument, with the font from the layout's configuration (a second
+argument names another font), so that an image can ship them and the device
+never runs pango. Its files are byte for byte what *--prerender* writes for
+the same screen, and the same bytes every time.
+
+The file is named after the layout, the keyboard size in pixels, the surface
+scale and a hash of the font description and of the environment, for example
+_ipaq-240x100-s1000-9a6df02a2244f18a.cache_. It is replaced atomically (data to a
+temporary file in the same directory, flushed, then renamed over the name), so
+a reader sees the old file or the new one and a failed write changes nothing.
+It is written only when something was missing, never touched in normal use, and
+costs no flash wear. If the directory cannot be written, the keyboard works
+as before and says so once on standard error.
+
+The environment in the key is what else the masks depend on: the versions of
+pango, cairo, harfbuzz and fontconfig, and the font file the description
+resolves to, with its path, size and modification time (the first face only; a
+fallback font for a missing glyph is not followed). After a library or font package update the key changes, the old
+file is not used and a new one is written beside it; the old one is never
+removed by the keyboard. Finding the font file starts fontconfig, so a start
+with a usable file is cheaper than one without, but not free. A file made on
+another machine, for example by *wvkbd-cache-gen*, is therefore only used where
+the same library versions and the same font file (path, size and time) are
+installed.
+
+A file is used only if everything in it checks out: the header, the checksum
+and every offset and size against the size of the file, and the layout, its
+version (derived from the label text), keyboard size, scale, font, environment
+and mask depth it was made for. Anything else is a miss: the labels are rendered and the file
+is written again.
+
+All integers in the file are little endian. It consists of
+
+- a header of 216 bytes: the 8 byte magic (the letters WVKBDLC and a byte 1), the
+  format version (2), the header size, the file size, a CRC-32 of the whole
+  file, the layout version, the keyboard width, height and scale times 1000, the
+  mask depth in bits per pixel, the label count, a hash of the font
+  description and the environment, the layout name (32 bytes) and the font description (128
+  bytes);
+- an index of 24 bytes per label, sorted by key, which is a 64-bit hash of the
+  label and the size of the box it is drawn in: key, offset of the mask data in
+  the file, mask width and height (both 0 for a label without ink), bearing x
+  and y, and the width and height of the label's logical box;
+- the mask data, every mask at an offset that is a multiple of 4, rows padded
+  to 4 bytes. Labels with equal masks share one copy.
+
+There are no timestamps or addresses in the file.
 
